---
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { execution, returnedAnswer, artifact, chapters, workflow } from './model';
import './attention.css';
---
<section class="at-lab" data-attention-lab aria-labelledby="at-film-title">
  <header class="at-film-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Actual execution / 46 seconds</p><h2 id="at-film-title">Both ends of the same task.</h2></div><p>Codex runs in a terminal. The native app supplies its question interface. The selected layout changes the file that the waiting task builds.</p></header>
  <figure class="at-film" id="at-film">
    <video controls playsinline preload="none" width="1920" height="1080" poster="/media/attention/poster.png" aria-label="Actual Codex terminal and native Mac Attention card, followed by the generated workshop page" aria-describedby="at-film-caption at-participant">
      <source src="/media/attention/execution.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      <track kind="captions" src="/media/attention/execution.vtt" srclang="en" label="English" default />
      Your browser cannot play this recording. <a href="/media/attention/execution.mp4">Open the original film.</a>
    </video>
    <figcaption id="at-film-caption"><strong>35 seconds of synchronized live terminal and native card footage.</strong> Both views are focused crops from one actual Mac screen recording, at original speed. Then: six seconds of the actual page render and a separate five-second check in the updated Mac app. The card first arrived before this live view began.<p class="at-version">Live exchange: <strong>mac-v1.1.8 / build121</strong>. Final check: <strong>mac-v1.1.14 / build128</strong>, after the upgrade.</p><p class="at-editing">Narrow masks hide private workspace paths and the specific terminal hostname. No terminal text is replaced.</p></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="at-participant" id="at-participant"><Icon name="check" /><span><strong>Real Attention call. Real native answer. Automated participant.</strong> Intel Computer Helper chooses <strong>Session cards</strong> in the native UI. David chose the Mac capture route; the layout selection is automated.</span></p>
  <div class="at-chapters"><p class="eyebrow">Jump to a recorded moment</p><div class="at-chapter-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Recording chapters">{chapters.map((chapter, index) => <button type="button" data-attention-chapter={index} aria-current={index === 0 ? 'step' : undefined} disabled><span>{String(Math.floor(chapter.time / 60)).padStart(2, '0')}:{String(Math.floor(chapter.time % 60)).padStart(2, '0')}</span>{chapter.label}<Icon name="play" /></button>)}</div><p class="at-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-attention-status>Chapter controls pause and seek this recording. They send no new question or answer.</p><noscript><p class="at-nojs">The film’s native controls work without JavaScript. Read the complete sequence below; the recorded answer and generated page remain available.</p></noscript></div>
  <div class="at-film-links"><a href="/media/attention/execution.mp4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the full-size film <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a><a href="#at-sequence">Read the text alternative <Icon name="arrow-down" /></a></div>
</section>

<section class="at-sequence" id="at-sequence" aria-labelledby="at-sequence-title"><header><p class="eyebrow">The round trip, in words</p><h2 id="at-sequence-title">A choice in the app.<br /><em>A consequence in the terminal.</em></h2><p>This is the recorded outcome. The page does not run an agent, ask a new question or submit an answer.</p></header><ol>{workflow.map((step, index) => <li><span class="at-step-number" aria-hidden="true">0{index + 1}</span><div><h3>{step.title}</h3><p>{step.copy}</p></div></li>)}</ol></section>

<section class="at-choice" aria-labelledby="at-choice-title"><header class="at-section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">The actual native card</p><h2 id="at-choice-title">The question.<br /><em>The returned choice.</em></h2></div><p>The screenshots preserve the native pixels. The text alongside them keeps the question and result readable on a small screen.</p></header><div class="at-choice-grid"><figure><a class="at-native-image" href="/media/attention/question.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open full-size original native question"><img src="/media/attention/question.png" width="618" height="800" alt="Actual Mac Attention card asking which Lantern Makers layout to build, with neither option selected" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><p class="eyebrow">Original question</p><blockquote>{execution.question}</blockquote><dl class="at-options"><dt>Schedule first</dt><dd>Three wide agenda rows, with times on the left.</dd><dt>Session cards</dt><dd>Three session cards, side by side.</dd></dl><p>The terminal waits for the answer. Only Session cards has a recorded outcome here.</p><a class="at-inline-link" href="/media/attention/question.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the original capture <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a></figcaption></figure><figure><a class="at-native-image" href="/media/attention/answered.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open full-size original answered native card"><img src="/media/attention/answered.png" width="618" height="800" alt="Actual native Mac card with Session cards checked, Response confirmed, and Answered status" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><p class="eyebrow">Actual returned answer</p><h3>Session cards</h3><p>Intel Computer Helper selects the option and presses Send Answer in the actual native card. The shared Attention MCP returns that choice to the original caller.</p><blockquote class="at-terminal-quote"><span>Text from the actual terminal</span><p>You selected “Session cards.” I’ll build index.html from that reference and verify that all three sessions are present.</p></blockquote><a class="at-inline-link" href="/media/attention/answered.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the original capture <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a></figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="at-output" aria-labelledby="at-output-title"><header class="at-section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">A real file, created after the answer</p><h2 id="at-output-title">The chosen layout.<br /><em>Ready at every width.</em></h2></div><p>The same Codex task creates the final page from the prepared cards reference. Its badge names the choice; its footer says “Built after your native answer.”</p></header><div class="at-result-images"><figure><a href="/media/attention/workshop-desktop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open full-size desktop render of the generated workshop page"><img src="/media/attention/workshop-desktop.png" width="1280" height="800" alt="Actual generated Lantern Makers page at desktop width, with the Chosen layout Session cards badge and three side-by-side session cards" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>Actual browser render at 1280 px. The three cards show the three workshop sessions.</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/media/attention/workshop-phone.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open full-size phone-width render of the same generated page"><img src="/media/attention/workshop-phone.png" width="390" height={execution.artifacts['workshop-phone.png'].height} alt="Actual browser render of the same generated HTML at 390 pixels, with workshop sessions stacked in one column" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>The same file at 390 px. This is a browser render, not a phone-device capture.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="at-download"><div><h3>Keep the file the task actually wrote.</h3><p>Standalone HTML with an inline SVG lantern. The download preserves the original bytes. It is served as plain text here and saved as an HTML file.</p><p class="at-file-facts">{artifact.bytes.toLocaleString('en-US')} bytes · three sessions · checked at 1280, 768 and 390 px</p></div><a class="button button-dark" href="/media/attention/workshop-source.txt" download="lantern-workshop.html">Download the actual page <Icon name="arrow-down" /></a></div></section>

<section class="at-evidence" aria-labelledby="at-evidence-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">Follow the evidence</p><h2 id="at-evidence-title">One waiting call.<br /><em>One continuing task.</em></h2><p>The original CLI recording contains one task start, one layout question call and one task completion. The durable native request points to the exact owned pane, and the actual returned option matches the generated page.</p><a class="text-link" href="/media/attention/receipt.json">Inspect the public receipt <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a></div><div class="at-receipt"><p class="eyebrow">Actual Attention result</p><pre><code>{JSON.stringify(returnedAnswer, null, 2)}</code></pre><p>The result is the recorded shared MCP return. Opaque original identities remain private. The production session field is blank; no cross-system session ID match is claimed.</p></div></section>

<details class="at-method"><summary>Recording, delivery and version details <Icon name="plus" /></summary><div class="at-method-grid"><div><h3>What was observed</h3><p>Actual Codex CLI 0.162.0 used gpt-6.1-sol with high reasoning and the installed shared Attention MCP. The released native Mac app displayed the question and submitted the selected option. This path used the actual durable production card.</p><p>Mac card arrival was observed. APNs accepted the question and resolution pushes for five installations, returning HTTP 200. Provider acceptance does not establish arrival on every device; iPhone and iPad arrival were not observed.</p><p>The question arrived before the later live terminal attachment. The film begins with that original question still pending. Its first 35 seconds are continuous synchronized source footage, with focused crops that exclude unrelated inbox rows and narrow masks over private workspace paths and the terminal hostname. No terminal text is replaced. The next six seconds are a still browser render of the resulting file.</p></div><div><h3>Two app observations, clearly separated</h3><p>The pending original round trip was preserved on mac-v1.1.8 / build121 when the update instruction arrived. After completion, verified signed and notarized mac-v1.1.14 / build128 was installed and reopened the same persisted answered card. The final five seconds show that separate check; they do not repeat the original live exchange on build128.</p><p>Intel Computer Helper performed the layout selection under explicit authorization. The native badge’s generic “CLAUDE’S QUESTION” label remains in the real pixels; the caller here is Codex.</p><p>Private evidence preserves the CLI thread, durable request and exact pane correlation. Production session_id is blank, so the public receipt reports that limitation rather than inventing an ID equality.</p></div></div></details>

<aside class="at-distinction"><div><p class="eyebrow">Different paths, different demonstrations</p><h3>Attention carries a terminal task’s question.</h3></div><div><p>Here, the external shared Attention MCP creates a durable native card and returns its answer to the terminal. The separate Question Lab shows Claude’s built-in AskUserQuestion in native chat, using an isolated Simulator build.</p><p>A task question chooses what to build. Permission decides whether a tool may run; steering adds new guidance to a task.</p><a class="at-inline-link" href="/labs/question/">See the separate built-in Question Lab <Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></a></div></aside>
<script>import { initAttentionLab } from './controller';document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-attention-lab]').forEach(initAttentionLab);</script>
